1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,304
Friday, nine in the evening.

2
00:00:02,354 --> 00:00:08,640
Malika runs a small flower shop online, and tonight it sells forty bouquets.

3
00:00:08,690 --> 00:00:12,816
Her phone buzzes forty times: paid, paid, paid.

4
00:00:12,866 --> 00:00:16,224
Saturday morning, she opens her bank account.

5
00:00:16,274 --> 00:00:18,096
Nothing is there.

6
00:00:18,146 --> 00:00:19,968
Nothing went wrong.

7
00:00:20,018 --> 00:00:23,856
Her phone told the truth, just not the truth she thought.

8
00:00:23,906 --> 00:00:31,344
In Lesson 1, a payment crossed the whole system and stopped at one word from the buyer's bank: yes.

9
00:00:31,394 --> 00:00:35,136
This lesson is about what that yes really is.

10
00:00:35,186 --> 00:00:37,800
Every card payment has two banks.

11
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:43,536
Malika's bank is the acquirer: it signed her shop up, and it will pay her.

12
00:00:43,586 --> 00:00:49,632
The buyer's bank is the issuer: it gave the buyer the card, and it holds the buyer's money.

13
00:00:49,682 --> 00:00:55,992
Between them runs the card network, carrying one question: may this card pay this amount?

14
00:00:56,042 --> 00:01:01,584
The issuer checks the card and the balance, and answers in about two seconds.

15
00:01:01,634 --> 00:01:06,248
After that yes, has any money moved?

16
00:01:06,298 --> 00:01:08,120
That yes is a promise.

17
00:01:08,170 --> 00:01:11,864
The issuer sets the money aside on the buyer's account.

18
00:01:11,914 --> 00:01:13,736
But nothing has moved.

19
00:01:13,786 --> 00:01:16,424
The money moves later, and in bulk.

20
00:01:16,474 --> 00:01:22,448
Overnight, the issuer sends the whole day's payments through the card network in one batch.

21
00:01:22,498 --> 00:01:24,320
That is settlement.

22
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:28,712
Then the acquirer keeps its fee and pays Malika the rest.

23
00:01:28,762 --> 00:01:33,368
Her forty bouquets arrive as one transfer, on Monday.

24
00:01:33,418 --> 00:01:42,776
I think this is the most important idea in the whole course, and most teams building payments learn it the hard way: approved is not paid out.

25
00:01:42,826 --> 00:01:50,552
A system that pays the shop the moment it hears yes will one day pay for a payment that never settles.

26
00:01:50,602 --> 00:01:52,544
And the bank covers the gap.

27
00:01:52,594 --> 00:01:56,792
Sunday, a buyer writes to Malika: cancel my order.

28
00:01:56,842 --> 00:02:01,232
How the money goes back depends on one thing: has it settled yet?

29
00:02:01,282 --> 00:02:04,640
If the payment was made today, nothing has moved.

30
00:02:04,690 --> 00:02:09,584
The bank simply cancels the promise, as if the payment never happened.

31
00:02:09,634 --> 00:02:11,456
That is a reversal.

32
00:02:11,506 --> 00:02:14,120
Friday's payment has already settled.

33
00:02:14,170 --> 00:02:18,992
The money is in the batch, so it must travel back as a new movement.

34
00:02:19,042 --> 00:02:20,864
That is a refund.

35
00:02:20,914 --> 00:02:22,952
Mix them up and someone pays.

36
00:02:23,002 --> 00:02:28,736
Reverse a payment that already settled, and the buyer never sees their money again.

37
00:02:28,786 --> 00:02:34,208
Refund one that was already reversed, and the buyer is paid twice.

38
00:02:34,258 --> 00:02:38,192
That is why this product never uses one word for both.

39
00:02:38,242 --> 00:02:40,064
Here is how it looks.

40
00:02:40,114 --> 00:02:44,624
On the bank's payment page, the buyer confirms with a code from their phone.

41
00:02:44,674 --> 00:02:50,840
The page says "Your bank is being asked", then "Paid", with a reference number.

42
00:02:50,890 --> 00:02:54,008
That is the issuer's yes, nothing more.

43
00:02:54,058 --> 00:02:57,728
In the merchant portal, Malika opens that payment.

44
00:02:57,778 --> 00:03:05,312
She sees the amount, the bank's fee, every call to the card network, and "Refundable until" a date.

45
00:03:05,362 --> 00:03:13,832
She presses Give back, and the dialog tells her the rule in one line: today it is reversed, later it is refunded.

46
00:03:13,882 --> 00:03:20,216
The bank's staff see the same payment as Reversed or Refunded, never just "returned".

47
00:03:20,266 --> 00:03:24,080
Monday morning, Malika opens Daily reports.

48
00:03:24,130 --> 00:03:31,688
Friday is there: forty payments, the fee, and the payout that finally lands in her account.

49
00:03:31,738 --> 00:03:38,312
To recap: a yes is only a promise, and the money moves later, in one batch.

50
00:03:38,362 --> 00:03:44,320
So when her phone says paid, is the money in her account?

51
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:49,600
Not yet: when her phone says paid, it means the money is promised.

52
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:54,664
When the report says payout, it means: the money is yours.

53
00:03:54,714 --> 00:03:58,888
Next: what you may keep about a card, and what you never may.
